EN = {
82: "Which post exactly the Important Person held, and in what it consisted, has remained unknown to this day. It must be known that a certain Important Person had only recently become an Important Person, and until that time he had been an unimportant person. Moreover, his position was not even now considered important in comparison with others still more important. But there will always be found such a circle of people for whom what is unimportant in the eyes of others is already important. Besides, he tried to increase his importance by many other means, namely: he arranged that the lower clerks should meet him on the stairs when he came to work; that no one should dare to come to him directly, but that everything should go in the strictest order: a collegiate registrar should report to a provincial secretary, the provincial secretary to a titular councillor or whoever else was appropriate, and only in this way should the matter reach him.",
83: "So it is in holy Russia, everything is infected with imitation, everyone mimics and apes his superior. They even say that a certain titular councillor, when he was made head of some small separate chancellery, at once partitioned off a special room for himself, calling it “the Room of Presence,” and posted at the doors some doormen with red collars and gold braid, who took hold of the door handle and opened the door to everyone who came, although in the “Room of Presence” an ordinary writing table could barely be fitted. The manners and customs of the Important Person were solid and majestic, but not complicated. The chief foundation of his system was strictness.",
84: "{I:“Strictness, strictness and strictness,”} he used to say, and at the last word he would usually look very significantly into the face of the man he was speaking to. Although, in fact, there was no reason for this, because the dozen clerks who made up the entire governmental mechanism of the chancellery were in due fear in any case: seeing him from afar, they would leave their work and wait standing at attention until the chief had crossed the room. His ordinary conversation with his inferiors smacked of strictness and consisted almost of three phrases: {I:“How dare you? Do you know who you are speaking to? Do you understand who is standing before you?”} However, in his heart he was a kind man, good to his colleagues, obliging; but the rank of general had quite turned his head. Having received the rank of general, he somehow became confused, lost his way and did not know at all what to do.",
85: "If it happened that he was with his equals, he was still a man as he ought to be, a very decent man, in many respects not even a stupid man; but as soon as he found himself in company where there were people even one rank lower than he, there he was simply impossible: he kept silent, and his position aroused pity, the more so since he himself even felt that he could have spent the time incomparably better. In his eyes there was sometimes seen a strong desire to join some interesting conversation or circle, but he was stopped by the thought: would it not be too much on his part, would it not be familiar, and would he not lose his dignity by it?",
86: "And as a result of such reflections he remained for ever in one and the same silent state, uttering only now and then some monosyllabic sounds, and so earned the title of the most tedious of men. To this very Important Person our Akaky Akakievich presented himself, and presented himself at the most unfavourable time, very inopportunely for himself, though opportunely enough for the Important Person. The Important Person was in his study and had fallen into a very, very cheerful chat with an old acquaintance and childhood friend who had recently arrived and whom he had not seen for several years. At this moment they announced to him that a certain Bashmachkin had come. He asked abruptly: {I:“Who is he?”} They answered him: {M:“Some clerk.”} {I:“Ah! He can wait, this is not the time,”} said the Important Person.",
87: "Here it must be said that the Important Person was telling a complete lie: he had time; he and his friend had long since talked over everything and had long been interspersing their conversation with very long silences, only lightly patting each other on the thigh and saying: {I:“So it is, Ivan Abramovich!”} {M:“That's how it is, Stepan Varlamovich!”} But for all that he ordered the clerk to wait, to show his friend, a man who had long since left the service and settled at home in the country, how long clerks waited in his anteroom. At last, having talked his fill, and still more having been silent his fill, and having smoked a cigar in a very comfortable armchair with a reclining back, he finally seemed suddenly to remember and said to the secretary, who stood at the door with papers for his report: {I:“Why, there is a clerk standing there, it seems; tell him he may come in.”}",
88: "Seeing Akaky Akakievich's humble appearance and his old uniform coat, he turned to him suddenly and said: {I:“What do you want?”} in an abrupt and firm voice, which he had purposely learned beforehand, alone in his room and before the mirror, a week before receiving his present post and rank of general. Akaky Akakievich had already felt the proper timidity in advance, was somewhat confused, and as best he could, as far as freedom of tongue allowed, explained, adding the particle “you know” even more often than at other times, that the overcoat was quite new, and now he had been robbed in an inhuman manner, and that he was applying to him so that by his intercession he might somehow, you know, correspond with the Chief of Police, or someone else, and find the overcoat. To the general, for some unknown reason, such a manner seemed familiar.",
89: "{I:“What are you about, my dear sir,”} he went on abruptly. {I:“Do you not know the procedure? Where have you come? Do you not know how business is done? You should first have submitted a petition to the chancellery; it would have gone to the head of section, to the head of the department, then it would have been passed to the secretary, and the secretary would have delivered it to me...”}",
90: "{A:“But, your Excellency,”} said Akaky Akakievich, trying to gather the small handful of presence of mind that was in him, and feeling at the same time that he was sweating terribly, {A:“I presumed to trouble your Excellency because secretaries are, you know... an unreliable people...”}",
91: "{I:“What, what, what?”} said the Important Person. {I:“Where did you get such boldness? Where did you get such ideas? What sort of insubordination has spread among young people against their chiefs and superiors!”} The Important Person, it seems, did not notice that Akaky Akakievich was already past fifty. Therefore, if he could be called a young man at all, it was only relatively, that is, in relation to someone who was already seventy.",
92: "{I:“Do you know to whom you are saying this? Do you understand who is standing before you? Do you understand it, do you understand it? I ask you.”} Here he stamped his foot, raising his voice to such a powerful note that even someone other than Akaky Akakievich would have been frightened. Akaky Akakievich simply died away, staggered, trembled in his whole body and could not stand at all: had the porters not run up at once to support him, he would have flopped onto the floor; he was carried out almost motionless. And the Important Person, pleased that the effect had surpassed even his expectation and quite intoxicated by the thought that his word could even deprive a man of his senses, glanced sidelong at his friend to learn how he looked on this, and saw not without satisfaction that his friend was in a most uncertain state and was even beginning on his part to feel fear.",
93: "How he went down the stairs, how he came out into the street, Akaky Akakievich remembered none of this. He felt neither his hands nor his feet. Never in his life had he been so severely dressed down by a general, and a strange one at that. He walked through the blizzard that whistled in the streets, with his mouth open, straying off the pavements; the wind, in the Petersburg manner, blew on him from all four sides, from all the lanes. In an instant a quinsy blew into his throat, and he reached home unable to say a single word; he swelled all over and took to his bed. So strong is sometimes a proper reprimand! The very next day a violent fever showed itself.",
94: "Thanks to the generous assistance of the Petersburg climate, the illness went faster than could have been expected, and when the doctor came, after feeling the pulse he found nothing to do but prescribe a poultice, solely so that the patient should not be left without the beneficent help of medicine; and, by the way, he announced to him at once that in a day and a half there would certainly be the end. After which he turned to the landlady and said: {M:“And you, my good woman, do not lose time in vain; order him a pine coffin now, because an oak one would be too dear for him.”} Whether Akaky Akakievich heard these words, fatal for him, and if he heard them, whether they had a shattering effect on him, whether he pitied his wretched life, none of this is known, because he was all the time in delirium and fever.",
95: "Visions, one stranger than the next, appeared to him incessantly: now he saw Petrovich and ordered him to make an overcoat with some traps for thieves, who kept seeming to him to be under the bed, and he kept calling the landlady to drag one thief even from under his blanket; now he asked why his old dressing gown hung before him when he had a new overcoat; now it seemed to him that he was standing before the general, listening to a proper dressing-down and saying: “I am to blame, your Excellency”; now, finally, he even cursed, uttering the most dreadful words, so that the old landlady crossed herself, never having heard anything of the sort from him in her life, the more so as these words followed directly after the words “your Excellency.”",
96: "Later he talked complete nonsense, so that nothing could be understood; one could only see that disordered words and thoughts were revolving round one and the same overcoat. At last poor Akaky Akakievich gave up the ghost. Neither his room nor his belongings were sealed, because, in the first place, there were no heirs, and in the second, very little inheritance remained, namely: a bunch of goose quills, a quire of white government paper, three pairs of socks, two or three buttons that had come off his trousers, and the dressing gown already known to the reader. To whom all this went, God knows: about this, I confess, even the teller of this tale did not take an interest. Akaky Akakievich was carried away and buried. And Petersburg was left without Akaky Akakievich, as though he had never been in it.",
97: "There vanished and disappeared a being protected by no one, dear to no one, interesting to no one, who had not even drawn the attention of the naturalist, who never fails to stick a pin through a common fly and examine it under the microscope; a being who bore submissively the jeers of the chancellery and went to his grave without any extraordinary deed, but for whom all the same, though just before the end of his life, there flashed a bright guest in the shape of an overcoat, which brought a moment of life to his poor existence, and on whom afterwards misfortune fell as unbearably as it has fallen on the kings and rulers of the world...",
}
